---
/* Component — DisplayCardTitle
 * The card's heading.
 *
 * A flex row rather than a plain block, so a title can sit beside an icon or a
 * trailing element with the two baselines aligned. Content comes through a slot
 * for the same reason — a string prop would forbid the companion element the
 * row exists for. This differs from PageSection, where the title is a prop:
 * there the heading is plain text by design.
 *
 * Renders a <p>, not a heading. A card title labels a component; it is not a
 * step in the document's structure, and making it one flattened the outline —
 * PageSubSection also renders <h3>, so cards inside one read as its siblings
 * rather than its contents. DisplaySwatch reached the same conclusion for the
 * same reason. This leaves h1/h2/h3 to PageMainContainer (or PageHero),
 * PageSection and PageSubSection, which are genuine structure, and removes any need for a level
 * prop.
 *
 * Note the slot may hold only phrasing content — an inline icon beside the text
 * is fine, a block-level child is not, since the parser would close the
 * paragraph early. Switch to <div> if block content is ever needed; the rule
 * below would need its selector changed to match. */
---

<p>
  <slot />
</p>

<style>
  p {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--bravoixr-spacing-2);
    font-family: var(--bravoixr-font-heading);
    font-size: var(--bravoixr-text-context-title);
    font-weight: var(--bravoixr-weight-subsection-title);
    line-height: var(--bravoixr-leading-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--bravoixr-tracking-display);
    margin-block: 0 var(--bravoixr-spacing-3);
  }
</style>
